[36m(PPOTrainer pid=3924694)[0m INFO 04-13 00:22:17 ppo_trainer.py:897] [trace] step=5 reward=1.185 response_len=448 completion_len=991 total_len=1525 preview='systemYou are ChatGPT, a large language model trained by OpenAI.\\nKnowledge cutoff: 2024-06\\nCurrent date: 2026-04-13\\n\\nReasoning: medium\\n\\n# Valid channels: analysis, commentary, final. Channel must be included for every message.\\nCalls to these tools must go to the commentary channel: \'functions\'.developer# Instructions\\n\\nYou are a chemist analyzing drug molecules. Available features for the get_features and get_neighbors tools: molecular_weight, heavy_atom_count, heteroatom_count, logp, tpsa, h_bond_donors, h_bond_acceptors, rotatable_bonds, fraction_csp3, molar_refractivity, complexity, electronic, functional_groups, ring_systems, 3d_properties, structural_alerts, metabolites, pka, ionization, logd, solubility\\n\\n# Tools\\n\\n## functions\\n\\nnamespace functions {\\n\\n// Compute selected molecular features for a given SMILES string. Pass a list of feature names to select which analyses to run.\\ntype get_features = (_: {\\n// Input molecule as a SMILES string.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// List of feature categories to compute.\\nfeature_names: string[]\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Find nearest neighbors from the CYP2C9_Substrate_CarbonMangels training set with labels, similarity scores, and optionally compute selected molecular features for each neighbor.\\ntype get_neighbors_cyp2c9 = (_: {\\n// Input molecule as a SMILES string.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Optional list of feature categories to compute for each neighbor.\\nfeature_names?: string[],\\n// Number of nearest neighbors to return. Default 5.\\nnum_neighbors?: number,\\n// Whether to include task labels for neighbors. Default true.\\ninclude_labels?: boolean\\n}) => any;\\n\\n} // namespace functionsuserInstructions: Answer the following question about drug properties.\\nContext: CYP P450 2C9 plays a major role in the oxidation of both xenobiotic and endogenous compounds. Substrates are drugs that are metabolized by the enzyme.\\nQuestion: Given a drug SMILES string, predict whether it\\n(A) is not a substrate to the enzyme CYP2C9 (B) is a substrate to the enzyme CYP2C9\\nDrug SMILES: CCOC(=O)N1CCC(=C2c3ccc(Cl)cc3CCc3cccnc32)CC1\\nAnswer:\\nPlease think step by step and use the relevant tools in your analysis. Then put your final choice, (A) or (B), after "Answer:"assistantanalysisWe need to determine whether the given SMILES is a substrate for CYP2C9. We can use get_neighbors_cyp2c9 tool to find nearest neighbors and labels. Let\'s do that.assistantcommentary to=functions.get_neighbors_cyp2c9 json{"smiles":"CCOC(=O)N1CCC(=C2c3ccc(Cl)cc3CCc3cccnc32)CC1","num_neighbors":5,"include_labels":true}functions.get_neighbors_cyp2c9 to=assistantcommentary{"result": "Nearest Neighbors for task \'CYP2C9_Substrate_CarbonMangels\' (k=5):\\n\\n1. Clc1ccc2c(c1)CCc1cccnc1C2=C1CCNCC1 (similarity: 0.5840, label: A)\\n\\n2. CN1CCC(=C2c3ccccc3CCc3cccnc32)CC1 (similarity: 0.4850, label: A)\\n\\n3. CCOC(=O)C(C)(C)Oc1ccc(Cl)cc1 (similarity: 0.2778, label: A)\\n\\n4. CCOC(=O)C1(c2ccccc2)CCN(C)CC1 (similarity: 0.2668, label: A)\\n\\n5. CN(C)CCCN1c2ccccc2CCc2ccc(Cl)cc21 (similarity: 0.2590, label: B)", "function_name": "get_neighbors_cyp2c9", "arguments": {"smiles": "CCOC(=O)N1CCC(=C2c3ccc(Cl)cc3CCc3cccnc32)CC1", "num_neighbors": 5, "include_labels": true}}assistantanalysisWe have 5 nearest neighbors with labels: 4 are label A (not substrate), 1 is B (substrate). The nearest neighbor (similarity 0.584) labeled A. Slightly high similarity? The second neighbor label A. Third and fourth A. Fifth neighbor B but low similarity 0.259. So majority label A. We might predict it\'s not a substrate. Let\'s also compute features for our compound to see if any important ones correlate.\\n\\nWe\'ll get features: maybe logp, tpsa, etc. Let\'s compute features.assistantcommentary to=functions.get_features json{"smiles":"CCOC(=O)N1CCC(=C2c3ccc(Cl)cc3CCc3cccnc32)CC1","feature_names":["logp","tpsa","rotatable_bonds","heteroatom_count","heavy_atom_count","molecular_weight","fraction_csp3"]}functions.get_features to=assistantcommentary{"result": "logP:\\n- logP (Wildman-Crippen): 4.89\\n\\nTPSA:\\n- TPSA: 42.43 \\u00c5\\u00b2\\n\\nRotatable Bonds:\\n- Rotatable bonds: 1\\n\\nHeteroatom Count:\\n- Heteroatoms: 5\\n\\nHeavy Atom Count:\\n- Heavy atoms: 27\\n\\nMolecular Weight:\\n- Molecular weight: 382.89 Da\\n\\nFraction CSP3:\\n- Fraction sp3 carbons (Fsp3): 0.36", "function_name": "get_features", "arguments": {"smiles": "CCOC(=O)N1CCC(=C2c3ccc(Cl)cc3CCc3cccnc32)CC1", "feature_names": ["logp", "tpsa", "rotatable_bonds", "heteroatom_count", "heavy_atom_count", "molecular_weight", "fraction_csp3"]}}assistantanalysisWe have a moderate logP (4.89), TPSA moderate (42), one rotatable bond, heteroatoms 5 (O=, O, N ring?), heavy atoms 27, weight 382.9, fraction sp3 0.36.\\n\\nWe should see patterns: CYP2C9 substrates often have MW 200-600, logP 1-5, TPSA 30-60, etc. Hard to decide.\\n\\nBut neighbor majority is label A. Probability: not a substrate. We\'ll choose (A).assistantfinal**Answer:** (A) – not a substrate to CYP2C9'
